# Overview
#
# `load-bearing` says that something depends on a part without naming
# what (`The nil-receiver guard is load-bearing`). The reader cannot
# tell what breaks without the part. When nothing depends on the part,
# the fix deletes the word. When something does, the fix names it (`The
# nil-receiver guard lets a caller with no compactor call Run`). The
# rule sits at Recommended, because the literal sense before a noun
# outside the guard also matches.
#
# Expression
#
# A space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so `[-, ( )]` takes the
# hyphenated and the spaced spelling alike. `literal` lists `wall`,
# `beam`, and `column`, and the `$` before each one expands it to every
# form, such as `beams`.
#
# The guard skips a word from `literal` after `bearing`. It sits glued
# to `bearing` and reads the token right after it, so a phrase at the
# end of a line also counts.
#
# False Positives
#
# The literal sense before `wall`, `beam`, or `column` passes (`Remove
# the load-bearing wall last`). The literal sense before any other noun
# still matches (`a load-bearing truss`).
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule did not hit any line in the measure.py corpus, so no sample
# measures its precision.
expr literal [$wall, $beam, $column]
expr main load[-, ( )]bearing!(( )@literal)
let message "`load-bearing`. Name what breaks without it, or delete the word."
let description "Flags `load-bearing` as a metaphor for a part that something depends on."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Remove the load-bearing wall last."
allows "Two load bearing beams hold the deck."
allows "The nil-receiver guard lets a caller with no compactor call Run."
